  "grafo_evidencia": {
    "nodes": [
      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "4b2f5a8e-2ff3-5325-b0bd-24eab5d94505",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Plants: Quesnelia, Billbergiopsis, Lonicera, Anisacanthus, Ancistranthus\n- Places: eastern Brazil\n- Concepts: endemic species, subgenera, common names, species counts, plant families\n\nFacts:\n- Quesnelia is a genus endemic to eastern Brazil.\n- Quesnelia contains 22 known species.\n- Billbergiopsis is a subgenus of Quesnelia.\n- Billbergiopsis contains 15 of the 20 described species of Quesnelia.\n- Lonicera is the genus commonly called honeysuckle.\n- Approximately 180 species of honeysuckle have been identified.\n- Honeysuckle derives its name from the edible sweet nectar in its tubular flowers.\n- Anisacanthus is commonly called desert honeysuckle.\n- The common name desert honeysuckle is also used for Ancistranthus.\n- Calling Anisacanthus a honeysuckle is a misnomer because true honeysuckles are Lonicera in the family Caprifoliaceae.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "9f7cf8a6-97f1-55c3-9bdd-7046ce42a43a",
        "text": "Page: Quesnelia. Sentence: Endemic to eastern Brazil, this genus contains 22 known species.\nPage: Honeysuckle. Sentence: Honeysuckles (\"Lonicera\", ; syn.\nPage: Honeysuckle. Sentence: Approximately 180 species of honeysuckle have been identified.\nPage: Honeysuckle. Sentence: Honeysuckle derives its name from the edible sweet nectar obtainable from its tubular flowers.\nPage: Billbergiopsis. Sentence: Billbergiopsis is a subgenus of the genus \"Quesnelia\" and contains 15 of the 20 described species of the genus.\nPage: Anisacanthus. Sentence: They are commonly known as desert honeysuckles, though this term is shared with the genus \"Ancistranthus\", and is something of a misnomer as true honeysuckles (genus \"Lonicera\") belong to the family Caprifoliaceae.",
